{
  "id": "lets-pretend",
  "title": "Let's Pretend!",
  "level": "A1",
  "summary": "Today you can be anything you want. Can you be a cat, a tree and a star?",
  "teachingMethod": "Drama-based",
  "teachingMethodReason": "Drama-based rather than TPR: the reader adopts and sustains a character across the whole book and speaks in role (\"I am a cat. I can jump high!\"), which is language produced AS someone else - the defining feature of drama-based instruction. TPR (see lets-move-with-milo) asks for a physical response to a command with no role and no speech, and needs a caregiver giving the commands. This is deliberately solo-doable: a child can read it and perform it alone, so unlike the TPR book it stays visible to children. Not story-based - there is no problem, no sequence of events and no resolution, only a series of roles.",
  "grammarFeatures": [
    "\"I am a...\" for taking on a role",
    "\"Can\" for ability (I can jump high)",
    "Imperatives (Be a cat!)",
    "Direct speech with quotation marks"
  ],
  "introFunFacts": [
    "🎭 Today YOU are the actor! Read the words out loud and BE the cat, the tree and the star.",
    "💪 Watch for the little word 'can' - it tells everyone what you are able to do!",
    "💬 See the \"quotation marks\"? Those are the words for YOU to say out loud."
  ],
